Use batched Kahn’s topological sort, keeping newly unlocked tasks separate until the current batch finishes. Sort each batch ascending; return null if not all n tasks can be processed. For n = 0, return [].

```javascript
function solve(n, edges) {
  const adjacency = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indegree = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [before, after] of edges) {
    adjacency[before].push(after);
    indegree[after]++;
  }

  let current = [];
  for (let task = 0; task < n; task++) {
    if (indegree[task] === 0) current.push(task);
  }

  const batches = [];
  let processed = 0;

  while (current.length > 0) {
    current.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    batches.push(current);
    processed += current.length;

    const next = [];
    for (const task of current) {
      for (const dependent of adjacency[task]) {
        indegree[dependent]--;
        if (indegree[dependent] === 0) {
          next.push(dependent);
        }
      }
    }
    current = next;
  }

  return processed === n ? batches : null;
}
```

Call solve(n, edges) directly; no imports, exports, or console I/O. With m edges, time is O(n log n + m) and space is O(n + m), including the result.

